Here, $f(x)=\frac{b-x}{1-b x}$ where, $0 \lt  b \lt  1,0 \lt  x \lt  1$ For function to be invertible it should be one-one onto.
$\therefore$ Check range :
Let $\quad f(x)=y \Rightarrow y=\frac{b-x}{1-b x}$
\begin{aligned}
& \Rightarrow y-b x y=b-x \Rightarrow x(1-b y)=b-y \\
& \Rightarrow \quad x=\frac{b-y}{1-b y}
\end{aligned}

where, $0 \lt  x \lt  1$
\begin{gathered}
\therefore \quad 0 < \frac{b-y}{1-b y} < 1 \\
\frac{b-y}{1-b y}>0 \text { and } \frac{b-y}{1-b y} < 1 \\
\quad+\quad-\quad+ \\
\Rightarrow \quad b \\
y < b \text { or } y>\frac{1}{b} \\
\frac{(b-1)(y+1)}{1-b y} < -1 < y < \frac{1}{b}
\end{gathered}

From Eqs. (i) and (ii), we get $y \in\left(-1, \frac{1}{b}\right) \subset$ Codomain
Thus, $f(x)$ is not invertible.

Since, $\quad f(x)=e^{g(x)}$
\begin{aligned}
\Rightarrow \quad e^{g(x+1)} & =f(x+1) \\
& =x f(x) \\
& =x e^{g(x)}
\end{aligned}

and

g(x+1)=\log x+g(x)

\Rightarrow \quad g(x+1)-g(x)=\log x

Replacing $x$ by $x-\frac{1}{2}$, we get

\begin{aligned}
g\left(x+\frac{1}{2}\right)-g\left(x-\frac{1}{2}\right) & =\log \left(x-\frac{1}{2}\right)=\log (2 x-1)-\log 2 \\
\therefore g^{\prime \prime}\left(x+\frac{1}{2}\right)-g^{\prime \prime}\left(x-\frac{1}{2}\right) & =-\frac{4}{(2 x-1)^2}
\end{aligned}

Substituting, $x=1,2,3, \ldots, N$ in Eq. (ii) and adding, we get

g^{\prime \prime}\left(N+\frac{1}{2}\right)-g^{\prime \prime}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)=-4\left\{1+\frac{1}{9}+\frac{1}{25}+\ldots+\frac{1}{(2 N-1)^2}\right\} .
